You are writing a prompt for an AI coding agent.
The prompt must describe, without including any code or code snippets, a Python
command-line tool that recursively finds PNG files and losslessly optimizes them
in place using the external `optipng` program, in parallel, with safety checks
and reporting.
Produce only that prompt, in English.
Write the prompt so the agent can rebuild the script from scratch.
Cover: - **Purpose:** A parallel, in-place PNG optimizer wrapping the `optipng`
binary, designed to run on Termux (Android) with a Termux-style shebang line. -
**Inputs:** A directory (defaults to the current directory) to scan recursively;
command-line options for optimization level (integer 1â€“7, default 7), number of
worker processes (default 8), per-file timeout in seconds (default 300), and a
dry-run or similar mode if applicable; plus any flags for passing extra
arguments to optipng.
Specify that the agent should infer any remaining option names sensibly and
document them in help text. - **Outputs:** Console progress and a summary of
files processed, skipped, unchanged, failed, and total bytes saved; per-file
error messages to stderr; an exit status reflecting success or failure. - **File
discovery:** Use `Path.walk()` (Python 3.12+) with in-place pruning of a fixed
set of directories (VCS folders, virtualenvs, caches, build outputs such as
`node_modules`, `build`, `dist`, `target`, and tool caches like `.mypy_cache`,
`.pytest_cache`, `.ruff_cache`).
Only consider files with a `.png` suffix, and verify the 8-byte PNG magic
signature before processing so mislabeled files are skipped. - **Processing:**
Copy each file to a temporary directory, run `optipng` with the chosen level and
a timeout via `subprocess`, check the return code against success status 0, and
compare sizes.
Replace the original only when the optimized file is smaller; otherwise leave it
untouched and mark it as no-change.
Use chunked byte comparison (64 KiB chunks) where content equality matters.
Clean up temp files with context managers, even on failure. - **Parallelism:**
Use `multiprocessing.Pool` with `imap` (chunksize 4) and `functools.partial` to
bind shared options.
Results are returned as a named tuple carrying path, sizes, captured stdout and
stderr, skipped/no-change flags, and an optional error string. - **Error
handling:** Handle missing `optipng` binary (check with `shutil.which` and exit
with a clear message), timeouts, non-zero exit codes, permission errors
(checking file mode bits with `stat`), files that vanish mid-run, and keyboard
interrupts (terminate the pool cleanly).
Never crash the whole run because of one file. - **Edge cases:** Empty
directories, symlinks (do not follow or should be handled explicitly),
non-existent or non-directory input paths, read-only files, zero-byte files,
files already optimized, and invalid option values (argparse validation for
level range and worker count). - **Execution:** Runnable as a standalone script
with the Termux interpreter shebang, using `argparse` with `--help`, `from
__future__ import annotations`, and type hints throughout, with `Final`
constants at module level.
No third-party dependencies beyond the standard library and the external
`optipng` binary.
Specify that the resulting script should be a single file, well-structured, with
a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":`, and that the agent
should preserve file permissions and modification times when replacing a file.
